{
  "_comment": "FINAL THESIS CANONICAL \u2014 May 1 2026. 56 cells x 3 iters = 168 runs. Both models (GPT-OSS-20B and GPT-OSS-120B-F16) under cgroup memory.max with --eager-compute on every uring/pin config. Multi-topic essay prompt (no '5000 words' sentence \u2014 gives 1999 eval_tokens on both models). All budgets and cache_slots derived per the April 12 eager-compute ceiling formula: max_slots = floor((budget - fixed_footprint) / slot_bytes_MiB) - 4 safety margin. Fixed footprint 20B = 6098 MiB (2385 pin + 3164 KV + 413 compute + 108 misc + 28 mmap). Fixed footprint 120B = 8164 MiB (2980 pin + 4635 KV + 413 compute + 108 misc + 28 mmap), all measured directly from /tmp/smoke_120b_20260501_185202/ logs. slot_bytes = 4.203 MiB on both models (n_embd=n_ff=2880 in both). Per-token working set: 20B = 4 experts x 3 proj x 24 layers = 288 slices. 120B = 4 x 3 x 36 = 432 slices. Policy regime per April 9 finding: cache < WS -> sweep LRU/LFU/LFU-aging; cache > WS -> LRU only. Only the 20B 7G cells fall below WS, so they get the 3-policy sweep on the 4 uring variants. All other uring cells use LRU. 120B at every budget here has cache > WS (12G c977 = 2.26x WS at smallest), so LRU throughout. Renamed flags: --uring-projection-overlap (was --uring-overlap), --uring-async-projection-overlap (was --uring-pipeline-v3), --uring-async-experts (was --uring-pipeline-v4). New code includes spin_check stack-local fix, parity-slot dispatch, parallel block-aligned quantize. Tracing disabled (--trace-mode off) so output disk I/O does not contend with model weight reads.",
  "models": {
    "small": "gpt-oss-20b-F16.gguf",
    "large": "gpt-oss-120b/gpt-oss-120b-F16.gguf"
  },
  "defaults": {
    "tokens_to_generate": 2000,
    "prompt": "Write a long detailed essay covering these five topics in sequence: (1) the history of computer science, (2) the history of mathematics, (3) the history of physics, (4) the history of astronomy, (5) the history of biology. For each topic, cover ancient origins, medieval developments, 19th century advances, 20th century breakthroughs, and modern applications. Include specific dates, scientist names, and their contributions.",
    "num_iterations": 3,
    "cooldown_seconds": 5
  },
  "paths": {
    "bin_current": "/home/keri/llama.cpp/build/bin",
    "bin_prefetch_on": "/home/keri/llama.cpp/bin_prefetch_on",
    "bin_prefetch_off": "/home/keri/llama.cpp/bin_prefetch_off",
    "models_dir": "/home/keri/llama.cpp/models",
    "results_dir": "results"
  },
  "io_monitor": {
    "enabled": false,
    "device": "nvme1n1"
  },
  "cpu_monitor": {
    "enabled": false,
    "interval_ms": 25
  },
  "blktrace": {
    "enabled": false,
    "block_device": "/dev/nvme1n1",
    "output_dir": "/tmp/bsc_blktrace"
  },
